# Claude — worktrees and local (machine-specific) settings stay untracked;
# .claude/settings.json IS tracked (shared project config)
.claude/worktrees/
.claude/settings.local.json

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.nox/
build/
# `nox -s build -- dist` (docs/release.md, lode-zuqp) writes here. MUST stay
# ignored: scripts/release.sh aborts on any `git status --porcelain` output,
# so an untracked dist/ from a local build would block the next release
# kickoff with a "commit or stash" message that `git stash` can't even fix.
dist/

# setuptools-scm build-generated version file (docs/release.md) — never committed
src/lode/_version.py

# Local data / secrets
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
.env
*.local

# Editor / OS
.DS_Store

# Beads / Dolt files (added by bd init)
.dolt/
.beads-credential-key

# Beads / Dolt files (added by bd init)
.beads/proxieddb/

# bd-dolt-push-guard.sh inputs (runtime, per-machine, per-DB-path — lode-fzau).
# Deliberately HERE rather than in .beads/.gitignore: that file is bd's own,
# regenerated by `bd init` (its entire history is two `bd init` commits), so a
# rule placed there can be silently rewritten away — the same hazard CLAUDE.md
# documents for the BEADS INTEGRATION markers, where lode's answer is likewise
# to keep our rules outside the regenerable region.
#
# Both MUST stay uncommitted; each is actively harmful if it travels, because
# the guard reads both to decide whether to BLOCK a push:
#   .bd-dolt-push-guard-highwater — this machine's last confirmed-pushed issue
#     count, written by scripts/bd-dolt-push.sh. A baseline for THIS DB path
#     only; committed, it would reach other clones asserting a count they never
#     pushed, and a WRONG baseline in a blocking guard is worse than the NO
#     baseline the guard already handles safely ("unknown" → don't fire).
#   .auto-import-issues.jsonl — bd's own marker that the local DB was hydrated
#     from a passive jsonl snapshot. The guard treats it as permanently
#     disqualifying for that DB path, so a committed copy would block EVERY
#     push on EVERY clone, forever, escapable only via
#     BD_DOLT_PUSH_GUARD_FORCE=1. It is bd's file and was inert before the
#     guard existed — the guard is what makes committing it costly.
.beads/.bd-dolt-push-guard-highwater
.beads/.auto-import-issues.jsonl
